# Every deployment target is Linux (the lab CCs, the standalone build boxes),
# and this is edited from Windows too — `text` alone lets core.autocrlf on a
# Windows checkout decide the on-disk line ending, and `git archive` (the
# deploy path: git archive HEAD | tar -x on the target) honours that same
# decision. On a Windows machine with autocrlf=true that meant every deployed
# text file came out CRLF, invisibly — first noticed on .py/.js files (which
# tolerate CRLF at the language level, so it went unnoticed for a while), then
# on files with no listed extension at all (VERSION, .env.example, a
# .properties.sample) once *those* got enumerated one at a time and still
# came out wrong. A per-extension list is exactly the wrong shape for this:
# it is only ever proven complete by the next file nobody thought to add.
#
# `* text=auto eol=lf` is the fix that does not need a list: every path git's
# own content-sniffing calls text gets normalised to LF, in the repo and on
# every export, full stop. Genuinely binary paths (fonts, images) are left
# alone by the same auto-detection — nothing below is needed to protect them.
* text=auto eol=lf

